ou can ask the  thousands of people  who have worked with me over my career  but how  i have conducted myself and i'm very  proud of that  fact and the fact is that's my greatest  source of pride the dignity and respect  with which i have treated others  and as a director of us citizenship and  immigration services  mr chairman as i mentioned before  i fix problems and sometimes fixing  problems  means making improvements and sometimes  making improvements means making change  and some people  aren't happy with change but change  improvement fixing problems is my  commitment  and i take stock in the fact that uh  just today an article was published uh  about me and um  i read in there uh mr homan's comment  mr holman was the leader of immigration  and customs enforcement during this  uh administration uh and he was the head  of enforcement and removal operations  when i served as a deputy secretary  mr holman and i disagreed on policy from  time to time but we had mutual respect  and what he said about me was the fact  that i always listened  that i always invited differing views  and i respected the articulation of them  i made my decisions  uh but i was fair and understanding and  open  and transparent in receiving and in  inviting  different views because that is an  obligation to make sure that we bring  the best thinking to the table on behalf  of the american people  i'm deeply proud immensely proud  of my conduct as a person and as a  professional  thank you that question wasn't  necessarily appropriate for a lightning  round i guess  um well i think we appreciate the answer  the question was perhaps my answer  wasn't  um i have to make a comment here with  regard to the ig because one of my  colleagues earlier  questioned the integrity of the ig and  as you know  uh mr john roth was the ig who issued  the report and substantially completed  it  his predecessor was the ig that my  colleague  was talking about and i just want to put  that on the record again n