rs of this committee to  make sure that all our borders  are protected uh against the challenges  that seek to do us harm  and that also the opportunities that the  borders present  in terms of the facilitation and  promotion of lawful trade and travel  are fully exploited for the benefit of  the american people  i commit to you that cooperation well i  appreciate that  uh and certainly the threats that we  face across all those borders  happen daily which is why this  appointment to this position is  absolutely critical to do it as quickly  as possible  we need to have steady leadership at the  department of homeland security  it's it's involved in what if you think  of all of the  demands that we have of government what  citizens expect from the federal  government i think it's safe to say  the number one priority for everybody  and the one that i believe passionately  is to keep us safe federal government  needs to keep us safe  that's the department of homeland  security is department of  defense and other uh folks who are  engaged  in in security but this position is  critical and hopefully we can fill this  as quickly as possible given of the  threats that we constantly face and i'm  just going to  close with one threat that i want your  sense of  your experience and your thoughts and  that is uh  biodefense we talk about a lot of other  security offenses but certainly  the pandemic has shown us the threat  that we get from  a biological threat that we have to  address that we have to mitigate  and federal preparations for disease  outbreaks also include things like  bioterrorism that could be used  by not a natural occurrence of  a bio threat like this pandemic but we  could be the subject of a bio-terrorist  attack  and there are other biological  challenges that all fit within the  department's responsibilities  but as you know biodefense uh like so  many of your responsibilities uh within  the  dhs it cuts across many different  federal agencies  so my question to you mr mayor is can  you speak about  what experiences you've had as a deputy  secretary that would inform you  as to how we should approach these  cross-cutting issues  uh like uh bio defense  uh uh thank you frank remember peters uh  let me  answer in uh in two parts number one  with respect to the department of  homeland security  internally within the department i had  the privilege  of getting to know the men and women in  the department  who focused on the particular threat of  which you speak  and i understand their qualifications  and capabilities  and it was um they were extraordinary  number one  number two i think you correctly point  out that this requires an  all-of-government  uh effort and it speaks to one of the  points i made in my opening  a remarks which is that fundamentally  the department of homeland security  is a department of partnership and we  must work with our federal partners  and our state and local tribal and  territorial partners to meet  the threats that we confront and that  includes the bio threat  of which you speak thank you mr mayor  chris  thank you for your willingness to serve  our country once again mr chairman thank  you  thank you senator peters uh mr mayorkas  uh thank you again for your testimony  this morning and we appreciate your  willingness to serve  especially at a time when our government  works to counter so many serious  threats and future threats as we've  talked about today  i also acknowledge that public service  is a family decision  and that involves family sacrifices so  we appreciate your daughters  and your wife being here today and  their support your decision to return to  public service  before we end today a few housekeeping  matters the nominee has filed responses  to biographical and financial  questionnaires answered pre-hearing  questions submitted by the committee and  had his financial statements reviewed by  the office of government ethics  without objection this information will  no